  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nA ridge remains in place over the southeastern US Friday and into\nthe beginning of the weekend. This ridge has kept things mostly \ndry but precip chances begin to increase Fri afternoon as the \nridge begins to weaken. The models are showing a significant band \nof moisture associated with a weak frontal boundary moving \nnorthward out of the Gulf and northern FL Fri. With the ridge in \nplace across north GA this boundary stalls across south GA but \ndoes increases precip chances across central GA due to its \nproximity. With the ridge weakening as we go further into the \nweekend a backdoor cold front moves SW out of the Carolinas and \ninto NE GA Sun afternoon. This front is fairly weak and loses a \nlot of its strength as it pushes into the old weakening ridge. \nSlightly cooler air is expected behind the frontal boundary on \nMonday, as a ridge of high pressure centered over Hudson Bay sinks\nsouthward. There still remains the potential for diurnal \nconvection, Mon/Tue with isolated to scattered afternoon/evening \nthunderstorms. \n\nLittle change in the temperature trends are likely through the \nweekend but will see slightly cooler temps Mon/Tue after the \nfrontal boundary passes through. Higher than average temperatures \nwill remain in place - with temperatures around 8-13 degrees \nhigher than climatology through Sunday. Daytime highs will be in \nthe upper 80s to upper 90s, with heat indices from around 90 to 106F.",
